# Neo Clipper parameters (docs/specs/mastering-tools.md §5.1). The order is permanent: songs and presets
# store values by position. Regenerate: python3 tools/gen-machine-params.py Machines/NeoClipper
namespace NeoBuzz.Clipper
class NeoClipperMachine
summary Clipper for drums, buses and masters: drive into a ceiling in dBFS, with a hard, soft (cubic), tanh or knee curve, 1× to 8× oversampling with antiderivative anti-aliasing, up to three bands (zero-latency Linkwitz–Riley crossovers, each band driven on its own), left/right or mid/side, mix, output, Delta (hear what it takes away) and a gain-match reading. No sample leaves above the ceiling at Mix 100 %; its latency is the same at every setting. Use it before a true-peak limiter to shave transients the limiter would pump on, or on drums for weight.

group Clip
Drive      "Drive"        0 360   120   db -12 24                          | Gain into the clipper in 0.1 dB steps
    how Raises the level going into the clipper by -12 to +24 dB, so more of the signal reaches the ceiling.
    sound More drive makes the track louder and denser, with more crunch as the peaks are flattened.
Ceiling    "Ceiling"      0 240   237   db -24 0                           | Where it clips, dBFS (0.1 dB steps): no sample leaves above it at Mix 100 %
    how Sets the level in dBFS, from -24 to 0, at which the clipper flattens or bends the signal.
    sound Lower ceilings cut the peaks harder, so the sound becomes quieter overall and more squashed at the top.
Shape      "Shape"        0 3     3     enum Hard Soft Tanh Knee           | Hard: flat at the ceiling. Soft: a cubic that bends from 0 and reaches the ceiling at 1.5× it. Tanh: the tanh curve, the ceiling its asymptote. Knee: straight up to Knee under the ceiling, then a quadratic bend into it
    how Chooses the curve that treats the peaks: Hard flat cut, Soft cubic bend, Tanh round bend, or Knee, straight until just under the ceiling.
    sound Hard is the edgiest and loudest, Soft and Tanh are rounder and warmer, and Knee stays clean until it bends.
Knee       "Knee"         0 120   30    lin 0 12 dB 1                      | Knee shape: where the bend starts, this far under the ceiling (0: hard)
    how Sets how far under the ceiling the Knee shape starts to bend, from 0 to 12 dB.
    sound A wider knee rounds the transition so peaks sound smoother; 0 dB acts like a hard cut.
Oversample "Oversampling" 0 3     2     enum 1× 2× 4× 8×                   | Oversampling, with first-order antiderivative anti-aliasing from 2× (1×: the curve as it is, aliasing). The latency is the same at every setting
    how Runs the clipper at 1, 2, 4 or 8 times the sample rate so the flattened edges do not fold back into the audible range.
    sound At 1x bright material can sound gritty from aliasing; higher settings sound clean.
group Bands
Bands      "Bands"        0 2     0     enum 1_band 2_bands 3_bands        | One band, or two or three split by Linkwitz–Riley crossovers (zero latency), each clipped on its own and summed, then clipped again
    how Splits the signal into one, two or three bands and clips each band on its own before summing them.
    sound With several bands a loud bass no longer drags the highs into clipping, so the result is cleaner at the same loudness.
Xo1        "Crossover 1"  0 10000 2594  freq 20 20000                      | Between the low and the next band (−6 dB here)
    how Sets the frequency, from 20 Hz to 20 kHz, where the low band ends and the next band starts.
    sound Lower values give only the deep bass its own clipper; higher values let the clipper handle more of the body.
Xo2        "Crossover 2"  0 10000 6667  freq 20 20000                      | Between the mid and the high band with three bands (never below Crossover 1)
    how Sets the frequency between the middle and high bands when three bands are in use.
    sound It decides how much of the upper midrange is clipped together with the highs rather than with the mids.
LowDrive   "Low drive"    0 240   120   db -12 12                          | The low band's drive against Drive (bands only)
    how Adds or removes up to 12 dB of drive for the low band, relative to Drive.
    sound Raising it makes the bass denser and louder; lowering it keeps the lows clean.
MidDrive   "Mid drive"    0 240   120   db -12 12                          | The middle band's drive against Drive (three bands)
    how Adds or removes up to 12 dB of drive for the middle band, relative to Drive.
    sound Raising it thickens the midrange and vocals; lowering it keeps them natural.
HighDrive  "High drive"   0 240   120   db -12 12                          | The high band's drive against Drive (bands only)
    how Adds or removes up to 12 dB of drive for the high band, relative to Drive.
    sound Raising it makes the top brighter and fizzier; lowering it keeps cymbals and air smooth.
group Output
Stereo     "Stereo"       0 1     0     enum L/R M/S                       | What is clipped: left and right, or mid and side (then left and right again at the ceiling)
    how Chooses whether the clipper works on left and right, or on mid and side.
    sound Left/right can pull the image sideways on lopsided peaks; mid/side clips the centre and the width separately.
Mix        "Mix"          0 1000  1000  pct                                | Dry/wet (the dry signal is delayed to match). Below 100 % the dry signal can pass the ceiling
    how Blends the clipped signal with the dry signal, delayed to line up with it.
    sound Below 100 % some natural dynamics come back, but dry peaks can pass the ceiling.
Output     "Output"       0 480   240   db -24 24                          | Gain after the clipper, 0.1 dB steps (the editor's Match sets it from the gain-match reading)
    how Adds -24 to +24 dB of gain after the clipper.
    sound It lets you match the loudness to the unclipped sound so you judge the tone rather than the volume.
Delta      "Delta"        0 1     0     switch                             | Hear only what the clipper changes: the output minus the input, delayed to match (gliding over 10 ms)
    how Plays only the difference between the clipper's output and its input.
    sound You hear just what the clipper removed or added, which shows what it costs the sound.

# What the clipper measures (NeoClipperMachine.Readings, machine_readings), over the last 3 s.
reading gain_match_db     dB    | Short-term loudness out − in (K-weighted, 3 s): what Output would have to take back for equal loudness
reading clip_db           dB    | How far the driven signal went over the ceiling, at most
reading clipped_percent   %     | Samples the curve bent (over the knee, or the ceiling in Hard)
reading latency_samples   samples | The clipper's latency, reported to NeoBuzz Live
